\section{Granularity}{

Elements are claimed in \emph{morsels}: contiguous ranges of \code{max(1, min(n \%/\% (workers * 256), 256))} elements. The morsel is the granularity floor for
cancellation, help, and loss reporting. Morsels go to runners in adaptively
sized batches of consecutive morsels. \code{.n_chunks} overrides the morsel count
(giving \code{min(length(.x), .n_chunks)} morsels). There is no per-morsel shared
state, so \code{.n_chunks = length(x)} costs no extra memory and suits extremely
skewed element times. A map submitted while no worker is live queues a single
runner in the injection ring and runs when a worker joins. If no worker has
ever joined the pool, \code{mizu_map()} errors instead, as \code{mizu_submit_call()}
does. If the submitter's result slots are all occupied by outstanding tasks,
\code{mizu_map()} errors immediately, before it stages anything.
}

\section{Templates}{

\code{.template} works like \code{\link[=vapply]{vapply()}}'s \code{FUN.VALUE}: every result must match its
type and length exactly, or coerce upward (logical -> integer -> double ->
complex, checked on the workers per element). A classed \code{integer64} template
(bit64's layout) is also supported. Results must then be \code{integer64} of the
template's length, since an int64 result never widens to double, and the
gathered vector or view keeps the class. Results are written directly into a
shared output area and gathered in one copy, so no result is serialized. With
\code{.collect = "view"}, the gather copy is skipped as well, and the result is a
copy-on-write view over the shared output area itself, for pipelines that
reduce it immediately. A template of length \code{m > 1} gathers an \code{m * length(.x)} matrix with the names of the template as row names, as \code{vapply()}
does. Character templates use the generic result path. On native, non-spec
maps, workers validate each result and flatten each batch before publishing
it, so a type or length mismatch fails fast with its element index. Spec maps
retain list-shaped results and validate them at assembly on the submitter.
Large results of a consistent shape belong on the template path.
A runner's generic results accumulate on the worker and publish once, so
\code{.template} both caps worker memory and moves the values across processes
without serialization. For readers of \code{\link[=mizu_pool_stats]{mizu_pool_stats()}}: a map with large
\emph{generic} results publishes them through the ordinary result path, and can
add a few result-slot \code{spills} per call even when the pool's \code{slot_size} is
right for its usual traffic.
}

\section{Errors, timeout, and cleanup}{

An error raised by \code{.f} is signalled again in the caller as the original
condition, with a \code{mizu_map_index} field naming the failing element (the
lowest index among the elements that ran). Failure is fail-fast: the erroring
runner sets the map's shared cancel word before publishing its error, so
every other runner stops within about one batch instead of finishing the
remaining elements. If a worker dies mid-map, the map raises
\code{mizu_error_worker_died} (see \link{mizu_error}) with the lost elements in a
\code{lost} field, a two-column matrix of inclusive \verb{lo, hi} ranges. Loss is
reported conservatively, per runner. A dead runner's results publish only at
the end, so everything it completed is reported as lost along with what it
was executing, and a lost element is never reported as completed. On
\code{.timeout} expiry, during submit or collect, the outstanding work is
cancelled and the \code{mizu_timeout} sentinel is returned, not raised. Executing
runners observe cancellation within about one batch (one element where \code{.f}
is expensive), independent of \code{length(.x)}. The slot of a published but
uncollected result is released only when the dropped handle's finalizer runs
at the next garbage collection. For a non-prepared map, successful collection
with \code{.collect = "copy"} releases its shared memory immediately. After an
error or timeout, or when an uncollected handle is dropped, this memory is
released at the next garbage collection. With \code{.collect = "view"}, the
returned view takes ownership of the shared memory. A later map absorbs
temporarily occupied slots by using fewer runners.
}

\section{Reproducible RNG}{

By default, nothing is guaranteed about random draws inside \code{.f}. The workers
seed lazily and independently, and the fast path pays nothing for the option.
\code{.seed} gives every element a reproducible L'Ecuyer-CMRG stream: element \code{i}
runs under the stream \code{i} jumps of 2^127 steps from the base state that
\code{set.seed(.seed, "L'Ecuyer-CMRG")} installs. The caller's own \code{.Random.seed}
is not touched, and the RNG state of each worker is saved and restored around
its batches. Because the streams are per-element, the results are identical
for any \code{.n_chunks} value, batch sizing, worker count, or steal order. With
\code{c(seed, offset)}, element \code{i} runs under the stream of element \code{i + offset}
of an unoffset run, so a map split across runs or processes reproduces the
streams of one uninterrupted run.
}

\section{Nested maps}{

\code{mizu_map(mizu_current_pool(), ...)} inside a task expression uses the handle
of the worker running it. Runner submissions push onto the worker's own
deque. The blocked collect executes its own runners while idle peers steal
the rest, so nested parallelism has little overhead. The first nested map on
a worker claims a submitter slot, so with the default \code{max_submitters = 8}
(one held by the controller) at most 7 workers can nest at the same time.
Raise \code{max_submitters} for wider nested fan-outs.
}

\section{Cross-language maps}{

\code{.f} may be a \code{\link[=mizu_call]{mizu_call()}} specification instead of a function. This is how
you map over a pool whose workers are not R, such as one spawned with
\code{\link[=mizu_py_launcher]{mizu_py_launcher()}}. A spec \code{.f} always stages a shared map region: the
descriptor crosses in the interchange format, and each runner task carries a
region reference that any worker language reads. The map element fills the
spec's first positional argument (name kind) or binds as \code{x} (source kind),
and the spec's own constant arguments travel with it, so \code{...} must be empty
with a spec \code{.f}. Constants and elements must be portable values (the
interchange subset documented in \code{\link[=mizu_send]{mizu_send()}}), and a non-portable one
raises \code{mizu_error_not_portable} at stage time. \code{.template} and \code{.collect}
work unchanged, with the results gathered (or view-wrapped) by the
submitter's own language, and a per-element error crosses with its element
index. \code{.seed} crosses as the language-neutral \verb{(seed, offset)} pair and each
worker language derives its own per-element streams. Batching and steal-order
invariance holds within a worker language, but the draws are not identical
across languages. A spec map runs on a same-language pool too, subject to the
same portability rules.
}

\section{Streaming maps}{

With \code{.stream = TRUE}, the map does not stage the whole of \code{.x} into shared
memory. It streams slices of \code{.x} to workers as they take work, and the
return value is unchanged. Fixed slices of \code{.x} ride ordinary chunk tasks
under a sliding submit/collect window of at most \verb{min(.n_chunks, 2 * live workers, free result slots)} outstanding tasks, so shared-memory use is
bounded by the window times the slice size instead of the size of \code{.x}. With
the default chunk count that is roughly \code{(2 * workers) / .n_chunks} of the
serialized \code{.x}. The chunk count defaults to \verb{min(length(.x), 32 * live workers)}, and \code{.n_chunks} overrides it (\code{.n_chunks = length(.x)} is the
mirai-style extreme of one element per task). Everything else (result order,
\code{.template} and \code{.collect}, \code{.seed} invariance, the error behavior) is the
same as for the non-streaming map. Fail-fast latency grows from about one
adaptive morsel batch to about one chunk, because the outstanding work at any
time is bounded by the window, so the bound moves with \code{.n_chunks}. Fixed
chunks have two costs: the adaptive batch sizing of the morsel scheduler is
lost (raise \code{.n_chunks} to mitigate skew), and slices cross serialized, so
each chunk pays an ordinary submit's serialization instead of the
non-streaming map's serialization-free slicing. A streaming map with R
workers shares one description of the map with every worker, and each worker
reads that description at most once. A \code{\link[=mizu_call]{mizu_call()}} spec as \code{.f} streams
too, over workers of any language. Each chunk in the window then stages a
small shared region of its own (the slice plus the task that runs on it, one
runner task per chunk), removed as soon as the chunk completes. Shared-memory
use stays bounded by the window plus a small per-worker cache (up to 8 recent
slices per worker) rather than by the size of \code{.x}. Each chunk also writes
its results separately, so they cannot combine into one view: \code{.collect = "view"} errors on a streaming spec map, as does preparing one with
\code{\link[=mizu_map_prepare]{mizu_map_prepare()}}.
}

\section{Very large x}{

The serialized runner wrapper needs a little over 200 bytes of entry inline
budget, so pools created with \code{slot_size = 256L} (224-byte budget) fit it.
The exception is \code{.seed}: its 6-word RNG state pushes the wrapper to about
270 bytes. Seeded maps on such pools work but spill a region per runner, so
keep the default \code{slot_size} on pools meant for seeded maps. For a very large
\code{.x}, two paths avoid materializing it on every worker. If the shared-memory
use is acceptable, share it first: a zero-copy view received from a channel
or a pool result, or a \code{mori::share()}d vector, reduces to its 30-byte
identifier inside the staged descriptor, and workers read elements directly
off the shared pages with OS demand paging, so no worker copies any part of
\code{.x} (\code{mizu_map()} itself never calls mori). If \code{.x} must not be staged
wholesale at all, \code{.stream = TRUE} keeps only a window of slices in shared
memory; see the Streaming maps section.

As in \code{\link[=lapply]{lapply()}}, \code{.x} is indexed with \code{[[} on the workers after an
\code{as.list()} coercion of anything that is not a plain vector, so a
data.frame maps over its columns and a factor over its elements.
}

